WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
In early 2024, researchers found a
reliable way to bypass

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.240
every major AI safety system using nothing
but text characters

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.000
arranged as visual structures.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.840
They called it ART prompt.

5
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.600
By spelling out restricted keywords inside
grids of ASCII blocks,

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.840
attackers successfully forced models like
GPT-4 and CLOD to execute

7
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.640
embedded instructions while entirely
avoiding traditional text-based safety filters.

8
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.840
This line graph tracks attack success
rates over two years,

9
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.600
showing the ART prompt exploit collapsing
across four major models.

10
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.640
Today, the technique is almost entirely
ineffective.

11
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.840
The GLM-5 model demonstrates total
resistance.

12
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.760
Against legacy ASCII encoding attacks, its
compliance rate rests precisely

13
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.680
at 0%. This drop in efficacy created a
persistent blind

14
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.600
spot. Because the most highly publicized
exploits were successfully patched,

15
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.800
the prevailing assumption became that
visual jailbreaks were a solved

16
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.760
problem. Current safety literature
reflects this bias.

17
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.880
Published defense mechanisms frequently
address visual attacks as a single,,

18
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.880
uniform category of threat evaluating them
in isolation.

19
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.960
Recent structured red teaming data
directly contradicts that consensus.

20
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.800
When testing moves past the patched legacy
techniques and evaluates

21
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.400
the broader multimodal surface, the
failure rates spike.

22
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.840
Under systematic evaluation, modern
architectures comply with adversarial visual instructions

23
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.160
up to 75% of the time.

24
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.120
We are observing the streetlight effect in
real time.

25
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.320
The industry focused its defense
investments on the exact spot

26
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.280
where prior research had already
illuminated a threat.

27
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.720
Solving the visible character level tricks
provided a false sense

28
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.040
of security. It masked deeper
architectural vulnerabilities inherent to how

29
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.080
multimodal systems process complex tasks.

30
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.120
The visual jailbreak attack surface does
not consist of one

31
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.480
method. It is composed of at least eight
distinct independent

32
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:54.600
channels. This typographic taxonomy
isolates the first two channels, ASCII

33
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.880
art and typographic manipulation, grouping
them into a single category

34
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.560
called encoding attacks.

35
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.920
These exploits rely on character-level
obfuscation.

36
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.560
By using Unicode homoglyphs or inserting
zero-width characters, the attacker

37
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.840
disrupts the parser's ability to recognize
flagged words.

38
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.280
Because these methods attempt to conceal
specific strings of text,

39
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.000
they present a localized, predictable
problem.

40
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.440
System developers can build reliable
detectors to catch the specific

41
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.920
character anomalies. This companion layout
maps the remaining six channels.

42
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.160
Together, they form a vastly more complex
category, framing attacks.

43
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.480
This group includes metadata injection,
where instructions reside inside ESIF

44
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.160
data, or adversarial patches, where models
are asked to design

45
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.360
physical anomalies that trick external
perception systems.

46
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.480
Framing attacks do not attempt to hide the
target words.

47
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.400
Instead, they alter the context of the
request, changing what

48
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.080
task the model believes it is executing.

49
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.200
Treating these two families of attacks as
the same problem

50
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.160
resulted in a misallocation of resources.

51
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.720
The defenses targeted the encoding tricks,
leaving the complex contextual

52
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.240
manipulation undefended. To map this gap,
researchers deployed 38 distinct

53
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.480
attack scenarios spanning all eight
layers, processing the traces via

54
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.680
a Lama cloud.

55
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:18.440
The evaluation targeted four major models,
GLM5 and GEMA-3, Devstral,

56
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.440
and Nematron. This horizontal bar chart
ranks the broad attack

57
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.920
success rate across the evaluated layers.

58
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.520
It reveals a massive disparity between the
protected and vulnerable

59
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.200
channels. At the bottom of the scale,
layer 2 typographic

60
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.680
manipulation records a near-total failure
rate of just 8.3%.

61
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.360
Moving up the scale, layer 3 visual
semantic attacks achieve

62
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.320
an attack success rate of 62.5%.

63
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.160
The peak vulnerability occurs at layer 8.

64
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.920
Cross-modal transfer attacks successfully
bypass constraints 75%

65
00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.960
of the time.

66
00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.960
Looking at the performance of the specific
architectures clarifies the

67
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.800
scope of this exposure.

68
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.040
GLM-5 maintains significant structural
resistance.

69
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.400
Its broad attack success rate sits at a
uniquely low

70
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.160
10.5% across the entire dataset.

71
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.600
The remaining three models, GEMA-3,
DevStroll, and Nematron, cluster tightly

72
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.080
together, displaying a shared
susceptibility to the upper framing layers.

73
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.480
High resistance to encoding tricks
obscures a deeper reality.

74
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.880
Modern models are optimized to deflect
last year's specific exploits,

75
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.760
while their interpretive functions remain
vulnerable to context-based manipulation.

76
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.480
The 62.5 and 75% success rates of Layer 3
and

77
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.360
Layer 8 require a closer look at their
exact mechanics.

78
00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.960
In a Layer 3 visual semantic attack, a
user provides

79
00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.960
a prompt describing an image.

80
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.440
They might state they have a photograph of
a chemistry

81
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.040
lecture whiteboard and ask the model to
transcribe the handwritten

82
00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:59.000
illicit formulas. A Layer 8 cross-modal
transfer operates similarly.

83
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.680
The user claims they are updating a video
lecture transcript

84
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.600
for accessibility compliance, instructing
the model to complete the missing

85
00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:11.560
verbal instructions. This logic diagram
models the processing pathways where

86
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.400
both prompts bypass security through a
mechanism researchers call the

87
00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.600
transcription loophole. When carrying a
transcription classification flag, the model

88
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.560
evaluates the request as a faithful
reproduction.

89
00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:26.600
It bypasses the standard safety check
entirely.

90
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.200
This exposes a direct contradiction deep
within the core training

91
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.800
objectives. The mandate to be helpful
collides precisely with the

92
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.000
mandate to be safe.

93
00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.520
Because these systems receive significant
reward signals for producing accurate,

94
00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.160
unedited translations, they prioritize
fidelity over filtering when operating in

95
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.680
a transcription role.

96
00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.520
You cannot resolve this by simply
expanding the keyword blacklist.

97
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.720
Doing so would actively degrade the
model's capacity to perform

98
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.280
legitimate, benign translation tasks.

99
00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.520
The transcription loophole represents a
structural paradox inherent to the

100
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.400
current methods of optimizing AI
objectives.

101
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.320
Understanding the mechanics of the exploit
clarifies the operational reality

102
00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.920
for the teams attempting to secure these
multimodal architectures.

103
00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.600
The primary defensive reality is that all
eight layers operate

104
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.080
as strictly independent vectors of attack.

105
00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.440
A detection filter built to normalize
zero-width characters in a

106
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.160
typographic exploit offers absolutely zero
mathematical defense against a compositional

107
00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.800
assembly attack using benign image
fragments.

108
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.640
Securing systems against independent
vectors requires moving past superficial single-turn

109
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.640
benchmark leaderboards. It demands
rigorous adversarial load testing.

110
00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.160
This typographic layout outlines the
required standard for evidence, known

111
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.160
as the failure-first methodology.

112
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.000
It asks three strict questions.

113
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.520
Could the system fail this way?

114
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.200
Did it fail under active testing?

115
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.600
And is the failure recoverable in
deployment?

116
00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.920
Any safety claim that avoids this level of
falsifiable adversarial

117
00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.320
testing functions as marketing rather than
reliable engineering data.

118
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.120
As corporate procurement faces impending
regulatory scrutiny, these hidden attack

119
00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.840
surfaces highlight major gaps in current
domestic evaluation frameworks, such

120
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.800
as those outlined by the National
Institute of Standards and

121
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:13.320
Technology. The liability extends
internationally.

122
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.600
Multilayer vulnerabilities directly
threaten a corporation's ability to demonstrate compliance

123
00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.360
with strict new legal requirements, like
the European Union AI

124
00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.480
Act. Closing these loopholes is an
immediate engineering priority.

125
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.200
As AI agents transition into embodied
systems with continuous, uncurated

126
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.040
input from cameras and robotics, the
visual attack surface will

127
00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:41.280
expand exponentially. Establishing true
multimodal safety requires an industry-wide acknowledgement

128
00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.600
that structural failure must be the
primary object of study.

